{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.RothHeightBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.RothHeightBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Polynomial.GaussLemma","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Degree.Operations","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Taylor","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Div"],"declarations":20,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.max_abs_pow_le_of_abs_coeff_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.max_abs_pow_le_of_abs_coeff_le","kind":"theorem","line":341,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L341-L350","doc":"**§10 verbatim: `H^j ≤ |A| ≤ a`.**\n\n`|A|` is Mahler's height — the sup norm on coefficients — so the hypothesis is exactly\n`InRothPolySet`'s third clause at one variable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.max_abs_pow_le_max_abs_trailingCoeff_leadingCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.max_abs_pow_le_max_abs_trailingCoeff_leadingCoeff","kind":"theorem","line":314,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L314-L339","doc":"**§10's `H^j`, both halves assembled.**\n\n`H = max(|p|, |q|)` is the height of `κ = p/q`, and each of the two coefficients §10 names is\none of `P`'s coefficients, so the max of their absolute values is bounded by any bound on all\nof them.  This is the inequality §10 turns into `Θ₁ ≤ log a / (r log H)`.\n\nThe `p = 0` corner is genuine mathematics, not bookkeeping: `κ = 0` offers no `P^j`\ndivisibility at all, and the bound survives only because coprimality then forces `q = 1`, so\n`H = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L305-L312","doc":"The trailing half from vanishing to order `k` — the mirror of\n`pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero`, off the same shared divisibility. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_map_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_map_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L297-L303","doc":"The trailing half over `ℚ`, via the same one-sided Gauss bridge as the leading half. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_linear_pow_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_trailingCoeff_of_linear_pow_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":280,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L280-L295","doc":"**§10's lowest-coefficient bound.**  If `(qX − p)^k ∣ P` in `ℤ[X]` with `p ≠ 0` and\n`P ≠ 0`, then `p^k` divides — hence `|p|^k` bounds — `P`'s lowest non-zero coefficient.\n\n`(−p)^k` and `p^k` differ by the unit `(−1)^k`, which is why the sign Mahler suppresses costs\nnothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.trailingCoeff_qX_sub_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.trailingCoeff_qX_sub_C","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L267-L278","doc":"The trailing coefficient of `q·X − p` is `−p`, when `p ≠ 0`.\n\nContrast `leadingCoeff_qX_sub_C`, which needs `q ≠ 0` instead: each end of the polynomial is\ncarried by its own coefficient, so each half of §10 has its own non-vanishing hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.trailingCoeff_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.trailingCoeff_pow","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L260-L265","doc":"Trailing coefficients are multiplicative on powers. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.trailingCoeff_dvd_of_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.trailingCoeff_dvd_of_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":250,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L250-L258","doc":"In a domain, a divisor's trailing coefficient divides the trailing coefficient.\n\nThe mirror of `leadingCoeff_dvd_of_dvd`, and equally cheap: `trailingCoeff` is multiplicative\nfor the same reason `leadingCoeff` is. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":213,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L213-L225","doc":"**The `m = 1` base case of Roth's lemma, complete.**\n\nIf `P ∈ ℤ[X]` vanishes to order `k` at `p/q` — every coefficient of the translate below `k`\nis zero — with `p, q` coprime and `q > 0`, then `q^k ≤ |lead P|`.\n\nWith Roth's hypothesis `H(P) ≤ q^{εd}` this gives `q^{θd} ≤ q^{εd}`, i.e. the index `θ ≤ ε`.\nNo Mahler measure anywhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.map_qX_sub_C_pow_dvd_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.map_qX_sub_C_pow_dvd_of_taylor_coeff_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L187-L211","doc":"**Vanishing to order `k` at `p/q` is divisibility by `(qX − p)^k`, over `ℚ`.**\n\n`(qX − p)^k = q^k · (X − p/q)^k` and `C q ^ k` is a unit in `ℚ[X]`, so the two divisibilities\nare one statement downstairs.  Extracted because **both** halves of §10 — the leading and the\ntrailing coefficient — consume exactly this and nothing else. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.sub_C_pow_dvd_iff_X_pow_dvd_taylor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.sub_C_pow_dvd_iff_X_pow_dvd_taylor","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L173-L185","doc":"**Vanishing to order `k` at `a` is divisibility by `(X − a)^k`**, read off the translate.\n\n`taylor a P` is `P(X + a)`, so its low coefficients are exactly the derivatives at `a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.taylor_neg_X_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.taylor_neg_X_pow","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L167-L171","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.taylor_sub_C_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.taylor_sub_C_pow","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L161-L165","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_map_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_map_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L138-L150","doc":"**The `m = 1` base case of Roth's lemma, assembled.**\n\nIf `(qX − p)^k` divides `P` *over `ℚ`* — which is what \"`P` vanishes to order `k` at `p/q`\"\ngives — with `p, q` coprime and `q > 0`, then `q^k ≤ |lead P|`.\n\nGauss moves the divisibility into `ℤ[X]`, where the leading coefficient argument applies.\nNo Mahler measure, no analysis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.IsPrimitive.pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.IsPrimitive.pow","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L125-L136","doc":"Powers of a primitive polynomial are primitive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.dvd_of_isPrimitive_of_map_dvd_map","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.dvd_of_isPrimitive_of_map_dvd_map","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L103-L123","doc":"**Gauss, one-sided.**  A *primitive* divisor over `ℚ` is a divisor over `ℤ`, with no\nprimitivity hypothesis on the dividend.\n\nMathlib's `IsPrimitive.dvd_of_fraction_map_dvd_fraction_map` requires **both** polynomials\nprimitive, which the auxiliary polynomial of Roth's lemma is not.  Splitting off the content\nremoves that hypothesis: the content is a unit downstairs, so it cancels. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.isPrimitive_qX_sub_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.isPrimitive_qX_sub_C","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L86-L101","doc":"`q·X − p` is primitive when `p` and `q` are coprime: any constant dividing it divides\nboth coefficients. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_linear_pow_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.pow_le_abs_leadingCoeff_of_linear_pow_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L64-L78","doc":"**The `m = 1` height bound for Roth's lemma, proved elementarily.**\n\nIf `(qX − p)^k` divides `P` in `ℤ[X]` and `P ≠ 0`, then `q^k ≤ |lead P|`, hence `q^k ≤ H(P)`\nfor any height that dominates the leading coefficient.\n\nThis is exactly what the base case of Roth's lemma consumes: with the hypothesis\n`H(P) ≤ q^{εd}` it gives `q^{θd} ≤ q^{εd}`, so the index `θ` is at most `ε`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.leadingCoeff_qX_sub_C","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.leadingCoeff_qX_sub_C","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L57-L62","doc":"The leading coefficient of `q·X − p` is `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.leadingCoeff_dvd_of_dvd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.leadingCoeff_dvd_of_dvd","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/RothHeightBound.lean#L50-L55","doc":"In a domain, a divisor's leading coefficient divides the leading coefficient. ","signatureChunk":0}]}